(CITATION: Kalin v. Campbell, 2014 BCCA 441 | DOCKET: CA041782 | DATE: 20141118) Tj
T*
(On appeal from: An order of the Supreme Court of British Columbia, entered March 14, 2014 \(2014 BCSC 412\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(REASONS FOR JUDGMENT OF THE COURT:) Tj
T*
([1] This appeal concerns the scope of pre-trial document disclosure under Supreme Court Civil Rule 7-1, and the) Tj
T*
(    proper test to be applied when a party seeks supplementary production of underlying financial records in a) Tj
T*
(    commercial joint venture dispute.) Tj
T*
([2] Rule 7-1\(1\) requires a party to deliver a list of all documents that are or have been in the party's possession) Tj
T*
(    or control and that could be used by any party at trial to prove or disprove a material fact. Where an opposing) Tj
T*
(    party establishes that the list is incomplete, Rule 7-1\(11\) and Rule 7-1\(18\) grant the court broad authority to) Tj
T*
(    order a supplementary list and production for inspection.) Tj
T*
([3] The associate judge below held that the plaintiff was obliged to accept summary spreadsheet reports prepared by) Tj
T*
(    the defendant's internal bookkeeper, reasoning that unredacted raw bank records were commercially sensitive.) Tj
T*
(    We respectfully disagree.) Tj
ET
endstream
